     ggml_ov_meansq.xml: the GPU CustomLayer descriptor for mode 9.

     NOTE FOR ANYONE EDITING THE PROSE HERE. XML forbids a double hyphen inside
     a comment, so none of these notes may contain one. It is not a style
     choice; it makes the file unparseable, and the plugin reports that as a
     failure to bind rather than as a bad descriptor.

     Reached by GGML_OPENVINO_CUSTOM_KERNELS=<path to this file>, which
     ggml_openvino_get_device_config() turns into the GPU plugin's CONFIG_FILE
     property. <Source filename=> is resolved RELATIVE TO THIS FILE, so the .cl
     must sit beside it; do not move one without the other.

     BINDING IS BY OP TYPE NAME. The plugin matches name= below against
     op->get_type_info().name and calls CreateCustomOp before its own op
     factory, which is why GgmlMeanSq (an op type OpenVINO has never heard of)
     reaches the device at all. openvino/op/rms_norm.cpp declares the op under
     exactly this name; the two strings are one contract, and changing either
     alone silently drops the graph back to the plugin's own path.

     ONE DESCRIPTOR COVERS EVERY RMS NODE. Job 372899 bound variant 1, which
     establishes that INPUT0_DIMS, INPUT0_TYPE and OUTPUT0_TYPE all exist under
     those spellings in this build. K therefore arrives per node as
     INPUT0_DIMS[3] and the element types arrive as the plugin's own, so the
     2560, 6912 and 1152 wide norms all share this one entry. Had only the
     all-static variant bound, this file would have had to be generated per
     distinct K at runtime.

     WHY VARIANT 1. It follows the plugin's own element types through
     INPUT0_TYPE / INPUT1_TYPE / OUTPUT0_TYPE rather than hardcoding them, which
     is what lets this op carry whatever precision the surrounding graph settled
     on instead of forcing a boundary.

     WHY THE ENTRY POINT IS ggml_ov_rrms AND NOT ggml_ov_meansq. Two jobs
     measured the answer. Binding the mean of squares needs an f32 output,
     because job 372912 found that mean(x^2) OVERFLOWS f16 on this model: the
     chunk spread went to exactly 0.00000, the signature of mean = inf reaching
     1/sqrt(inf) = 0. And job 372911 found what an f32 output then costs, since
     726 opaque f32 custom ops inside an f16 graph disrupt the plugin's
     precision plan badly enough to add 8.77 SECONDS of full size reorders
     against the 47 ms being chased.

     The reciprocal RMS escapes both. It runs about 1e-3 to 1e-1 here, far from
     f16's 65504 ceiling and equally far from its 6e-8 subnormal floor, so the
     output can follow the input type and no boundary appears. It is also
     exactly what the plugin's own rms_gpu_bfyx_opt stores, for the same reason.

     eps is the SECOND INPUT, not a <Define>. As a define it would have to be
     baked per eps value, which means generating this file at runtime; as an
     input it is one more constant the frontend already has, and one descriptor
     still covers every norm.
-->
<CustomLayer name="GgmlRmsRecip" type="SimpleGPU" version="1">
  <Kernel entry="ggml_ov_rrms">
    <Source filename="ggml_ov_meansq.cl"/>
    <Define name="MS_VARIANT" type="int" default="1"/>
    <!-- Entry 2 is ggml_ov_rrms. The .cl holds two __kernel bodies and cldnn
         asserts a program contains exactly one, so whichever is not being bound
         has to be preprocessed away. It defaults to 2 inside the file as well,
         but a contract this easy to break is worth stating in both places. Job
         372913 is what breaking it looks like: valid OpenCL C that compiles
         standalone and fails at ProgramBuilder with an error naming neither the
         file nor the surplus kernel. -->
    <Define name="MS_ENTRY" type="int" default="2"/>
    <!-- LWS is read twice: the kernel sizes its __local array and its
         reqd_work_group_size from this define, and the dispatch below takes it
         from <WorkSizes local=>. They must agree. A mismatch is not a build
         error, it is a wrong answer. -->
    <Define name="LWS" type="int" default="256"/>
  </Kernel>
  <Buffers>
    <Tensor arg-index="0" type="input"  port-index="0" format="BFYX"/>
    <!-- eps, one element. -->
    <Tensor arg-index="1" type="input"  port-index="1" format="BFYX"/>
    <Tensor arg-index="2" type="output" port-index="0" format="BFYX"/>
  </Buffers>
  <!-- CL2.0 is required: get_sub_group_id and get_num_sub_groups are OpenCL 2.0
       builtins, and without the flag every variant fails at
       CL_BUILD_PROGRAM_FAILURE (job 372897). A CustomLayer source gets none of
       the JIT preamble cldnn prepends to its own kernels. -->
  <CompilerOptions options="-cl-mad-enable -cl-std=CL2.0"/>
  <!-- One work-group per row. global is the OUTPUT element count times LWS, and
       the output is {..., rows, 1}, so B*F*Y*X is the row count however the
       plugin assigned the axes. That is what makes this need no per shape
       edit. -->
  <WorkSizes global="B*F*Y*X*256,1,1" local="256,1,1"/>
</CustomLayer>
